WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.640
This chart compares the spread of current
AI economic forecasts.

2
00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.840
On one end, MIT economist Darren Asimoglu
projects a 0.5%

3
00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.760
increase in productivity.

4
00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:16.720
On the other, PWC projects $15.7 trillion
in new value

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.600
by 2030. These institutions are analyzing
the same generative models

6
00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:24.480
and the same neural networks, yet they are
arriving at

7
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.800
two entirely separate realities.

8
00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.840
This massive variance isn't a math error
or a failure

9
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:33.360
of logic. The spread exists because these
models use different

10
00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:37.280
assumptions about what AI is, which tasks
it can actually

11
00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:40.720
handle, and how fast businesses actually
change.

12
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.280
The space between these numbers is where
the real data

13
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.800
lives. It provides a map of the specific
economic frictions

14
00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:48.480
determining the next decade.

15
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.240
In this blueprint, we see AI positioned
alongside the historical

16
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.800
electrical grid. Forecasters like Goldman
Sachs and McKenzie model AI

17
00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.440
as a general-purpose technology, a tool
that eventually changes the

18
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:02.520
cost structure of almost everything.

19
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.760
Their logic assumes AI will automate vast
categories of cognitive

20
00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.920
work, creating compounding productivity
gains that eventually show up in

21
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:15.480
global GDP. The counter model from MIT
challenges the breadth

22
00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:16.760
of that automation.

23
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:20.360
It suggests that most cognitive tasks are
far more difficult

24
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.240
to replace than the optimistic models
allow for.

25
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:26.040
When you filter the technology down to
tasks that are

26
00:01:26.040 --> 00:01:29.800
cost-effective to replace today, the
target area shrinks.

27
00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:33.560
In this model, AI only impacts about 5% of
total

28
00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.320
work performed by humans.

29
00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:39.480
This pits a model of compounding
multi-decade gains against a

30
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.360
model of restricted, near-term viability.

31
00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:46.520
The world's top economists are debating
the practical economic definition

32
00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:48.280
of the word automatable.

33
00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:52.360
We can deconstruct this divergence into
three specific variables.

34
00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.600
The first is the scope of automation.

35
00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:58.720
This measures whether AI takes over broad
roles, like accountant,

36
00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:01.760
or if it only speeds up narrow tasks, like
summarizing

37
00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:03.120
a meeting transcript.

38
00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.840
The second variable is the adoption rate.

39
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:09.680
Corporate inertia and workforce resistance
often act as a break,

40
00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:13.680
preventing theoretical technology from
becoming a practical reality.

41
00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.520
Regulatory hurdles and the sheer
difficulty of plugging new AI

42
00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.320
into 50-year-old legacy software systems
create significant delays between a

43
00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.280
tool's release and its actual use in
production.

44
00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:29.120
The third variable dictating the economic
reality is the profitability

45
00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.800
filter. This scale shows the actual math
of automation.

46
00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.120
Because AI requires massive capital
investment and ongoing compute costs,

47
00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:39.840
a task is only automated if the system
costs less

48
00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:41.840
than the human salary it replaces.

49
00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:44.960
Technological capability is secondary to
the bottom line.

50
00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.680
If the deployment economics don't work,
the task stays with

51
00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:50.680
a human. If you look past the headline
numbers, these

52
00:02:50.680 --> 00:02:54.120
competing economists actually agree on the
primary outcomes.

53
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.160
They all point to the productivity paradox
shown here.

54
00:02:57.160 --> 00:02:59.720
There is a five to ten year dead zone
where

55
00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:04.200
technical capability rises while the
actual impact on GDP stays

56
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.320
flat. There is also a consensus that these
gains will

57
00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:10.920
be uneven. AI is not expected to provide a
universal

58
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.920
lift. Instead, it will concentrate wealth
in specific sectors like

59
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:16.680
software and knowledge work.

60
00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:19.960
Within those sectors, the financial
rewards will likely flow to

61
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:23.160
workers who use AI to complement their
output, while those

62
00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:27.080
who compete directly against the
technology face declining wages.

63
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:31.080
Because the friction of adoption takes
years to resolve, organizations

64
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:34.360
have to begin building foundational AI
capability now.

65
00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.920
Companies that wait for the productivity
gains to show up

66
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.720
in the national data may find the
efficiency gap has

67
00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:41.880
already become too wide to close.

68
00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.200
We can act on the certainty that AI is
changing

69
00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.840
the cost of cognitive work while treating
the trillion-dollar forecasts

70
00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.720
as scenarios to be managed rather than
certainties to be

71
00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.000
expected.

